{:activity/_scope [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060675} :activity/id #uuid "5052a9d6-d9c5-5df7-993c-818d53b162fb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060938 :tutorial/content "We can use matrices to solve systems of linear equations. To do this, we need to set up the **augmented matrix** for the system.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the system of equations below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y=-1 \\\\ -3x+7y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the **coefficient matrix**, which consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we create the **augmented matrix**. This is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the future, we will learn how to use coefficient and augmented matrices to solve systems of equations. But for now, let's practice setting them up." :tutorial/id #uuid "36b1a271-77b9-59ed-9f12-1b6675c26830" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3146 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5b2031d1-6747-5c9a-b8e2-711f6dd1e094" :step/math-academy-id 17169 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060678}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059767 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059576} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb13e9d9-19f0-5f39-b9f8-e6e2f8fac433" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338975}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient Matrix of a System of Linear Equations"} :step/id #uuid "8e2bc116-2181-5120-9389-ff1ba9b3ee5e" :step/math-academy-id 17170 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059768 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059577} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f9ccaa5-d69c-584d-946e-0c57b3050160" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338976}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Augmented Matrix of a System of Linear Equations"} :step/id #uuid "e6584e81-18d3-5c82-b510-c6d84d369be3" :step/math-academy-id 17171 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060939 :tutorial/content "We will use mostly systems with the following variables:\n\n- $x,y,z,$ or\n\n- $x_1,x_2,x_3,$ or\n\n- $a,b,c.$\n\nThese sets of variables will always have a *natural* order:\n\n- alphabetical order in the case of $x,y,z$ or $a,b,c,$ and\n\n- numerical order in the case of $x_1, x_2, x_3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f6081e68-9014-5a0d-a6bf-084abe5fd28e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3147 :tutorial/title "The Order of the Variables in a Linear System"} :step/id #uuid "17bd5ecf-ddf0-5755-a7bc-19c589337468" :step/math-academy-id 17172 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059769 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059578} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7c8f4da-c782-51e0-ac51-87a89f31860d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262949} {:db/id #edb/re